---
/**
 * Applies the stored theme to <html> before first paint, and re-applies it after every
 * ClientRouter navigation.
 *
 * Shared by BaseLayout and AdminLayout rather than duplicated: the two had identical
 * copies of the bootstrap, and only one of them would ever have been fixed.
 *
 * Why the `astro:after-swap` listener is load-bearing — two ClientRouter behaviours
 * combine to wipe the theme on every client-side navigation:
 *
 *   1. `swapRootAttributes()` removes every attribute from the live <html> and re-applies
 *      the incoming document's. The server renders `data-theme=""`, so the attribute
 *      comes back empty on each navigation.
 *   2. `deselectScripts()` keys inline scripts by their text content and marks ones it
 *      has already run, so this script executes on the first load only — nothing puts
 *      the attribute back.
 *
 * Since dark is expressed *solely* as `[data-theme="dark"]` (there is no
 * `prefers-color-scheme` rule in the stylesheet), an empty attribute means light. The
 * visible symptom was a dark-mode reader being thrown back to light on every page they
 * opened.
 *
 * `astro:after-swap` fires inside the view-transition update callback — after the DOM is
 * swapped but before the browser paints — so re-applying there corrects the attribute
 * with no flash. The listener is on `document`, which survives swaps (only <body> is
 * replaced), so registering it once here is enough.
 *
 * `is:inline` and NOT `type="module"`: a module script is deferred, which would put the
 * first application after first paint and reintroduce the flash on full page loads.
 */
---

<script is:inline>
  (function () {
    var apply = function () {
      var stored = localStorage.getItem('theme');
      var theme = stored || (matchMedia('(prefers-color-scheme: dark)').matches ? 'dark' : 'light');
      document.documentElement.dataset.theme = theme;
    };

    apply();
    document.addEventListener('astro:after-swap', apply);
  })();
</script>
